<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apache</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apache are a group of culturally related Indigenous peoples of the American Southwest and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, speaking Southern Athabaskan languages. Arriving from the north by about 1500, they include the Chiricahua, Mescalero, Jicarilla, Lipan and Western Apache. For more than two centuries their raids challenged Spanish, Mexican and American frontier settlements, and leaders such as Cochise and Geronimo resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until 1886.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apache.jpg" alt="Apache" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robfergusonjr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apache_portraits.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Comanche became mounted bison hunters and drove the Jicarilla, Lipan and other <b>Apache</b> groups from the grasslands east of the Rocky Mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against <b>Apache</b> and Comanche raids, and the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of New Mexico until Diego de Vargas returned in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 1500 Athapaskan-speaking peoples, the ancestors of the Navajo and <b>Apache</b>, had entered the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their language, Diné bizaad, “the people’s language”, is a Southern Athabaskan language of the Na-Dené family, with two mutually intelligible dialects, and is closely related to the languages of the <b>Apache</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the reforms of José de Gálvez, Charles III separated the northern provinces of New Spain from the viceroy's direct control in 1776 to strengthen frontier defence against the <b>Apache</b> and Comanche.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>Presidios guarded routes against <b>Apache</b> and other raiders, and the Camino Real linked it to Mexico City and Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/state/nueva-vizcaya">Nueva Vizcaya</a></li></ul></section>
